Let me understand ticket 1:

1. Minimal playable snake: a bare-minimum but fully functional snake game with minimal styling.
   - Grid is drawn on a `<canvas>` element; board size fixed at the default 20.
   - Snake moves, direction changes via keyboard (arrow keys).
   - Food appears at random empty cells; when eaten, the snake grows and score increases.
   - Collision with walls or itself ends the game; game-over message and "Play Again" button.
   - Bare-minimum CSS to make it playable — no neon polish yet.
   - After this ticket, the model can open `index.html` and play a complete game.

So ticket 1 is: a complete game loop including start → play → game over → play again. The start menu (board-size input, high score, "press any key" overlay) belongs to ticket 2. But "the model can open index.html and play a complete game" — meaning, does the game start immediately on page load? Hmm. The ticket says "the model can open index.html and play a complete game". So probably: when the page opens, the game is ready to play immediately (or a minimal start). Since the start menu is ticket 2, in ticket 1 the game should be playable immediately on load — the snake moves, arrow keys work, food, score, wall/self collision, game-over message, and a "Play Again" button to restart.

Wait — but the ticket says "direction changes via keyboard (arrow keys)" — only arrow keys are needed for ticket 1 (WASD is a general requirement of the main prompt, but the ticket explicitly lists arrow keys). Ticket 1 only mentions arrow keys. Adding WASD is easy too, and it's in the overall spec — but the instructions say complete only ticket 1 and don't touch the other tickets. WASD control is part of the main task ("controls: arrow keys and WASD"). Hmm, but ticket 2 is "start menu + scoring system" and ticket 3 is neon polish. The main prompt lists requirements that span all tickets. To be safe, I'll implement arrow keys and WASD (WASD is a core gameplay control, not menu/score/polish) — it doesn't violate "don't work on other tickets" since it's within the scope of the gameplay ticket. Actually, let me think: the ticket explicitly lists only arrow keys. But the overall deliverable requires WASD. Adding WASD in ticket 1 is fine and does no harm. I'll include it.

Also, the score needs real-time display (the ticket says "eating food grows the snake and increases score" — the score should be visible). A minimal score display is needed to verify score increases.

Game over: message + "Play Again" button that restarts the game.

Board size fixed at the default 20 (no config yet — that's ticket 2).

Minimal styling: just enough to be playable. Dark or light? The neon style is ticket 3. Minimal styling: a simple dark background with basic colors is fine. Keep it simple: dark background, a visible snake, visible food, score text. "No neon polish yet" — so avoid glow effects and fancy fonts, but a simple dark theme is enough.

Architecture per spec §7: separate the game logic from rendering (a Game object with a render(ctx) method). That's a code-quality item in ticket 3, but it's good practice to do it from the start.

